.TH CRYPTO 1 LOCAL.SH NAMEcrypto \- the cryptogram puzzler's friend.SH SYNOPSIS.B crypto[-cegrsu] [file(s)].SH DESCRIPTION.I Cryptois a fun program for playing around with cryptograms. It can be used to:.TP 3.B  oEncode regular text with a random or user-picked cypher.TP 3.B  oUse pre-encoded files which might include the solution.TP 3.B  oInteractivley place letters into the puzzle.TP 3.B  oNon-interactivley encode/decode puzzle text.TP 3.B  oSearch /usr/dict/words for possible matches of a particular word.TP 3.B  oCreate/access multi-puzzle files.PPIn normal usage, text is taken from the specified file (orthe standard input), encoded (if needed) and displayed for you to solve. You can save the current puzzle at any time,and take up where you left off later on.  If the cypher ispicked by (or supplied to).I crypto\,it will inform you when you have correctly solved the puzzle.  You willalso be able to cheat and see the correct solution one letter at a time.A simple, yet entertaining example is the command:.spfortune -l | crypto -r.spwhich encrypts a long fortune with a simple roational cypher and lets yousolve it.  For a more difficult time, try:.spfortune -s | crypto -e.PP.I Cryptocan also be used in a non-interactive manner by redirecting the output toa file.  In this case, the text is read in, encoded if -e, -r, or -c wasspecified, and the resulting.I puzzleis output.  If, however, a guess wasspecified with the -g option, the guess is applied to the puzzle and theresulting.I solutionis output.  When redirecting, you will probably want to use the -n optionto avoid outputting the brace-commands (see below).  For example:.spcrypto -n -gzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba puzz >solved.PP.I Cryptouses a simple, text-based output format to save the puzzle, guess, cypher,and title in a file.  These take the form of "brace-commands", each on a lineof their own, interspersed into the puzzle text.  {-gabc...xyz} specifiesthe current guess, {-cabc...xyz} specifes the cypher, and {-tTitle} specifiesthe title.  Any or all of these may be ommitted to take on their defaultvalues.  In addition, the command {-end} allows you to separate the fileinto multiple puzzles, just as if you'd specified multiple file names onthe command-line.  Each puzzle has a unique guess, cypher, and title..PPWhen multiple puzzles are loaded, you will be presented with a menuof choices for your initial puzzle.  From there, the save command (^V)saves all the puzzles into a single file.  This menu can be visited atany time with the menu command (^M)..PPAn easy way to create a multi-puzzle file would be to execute the command:.sp        fortune | crypto -e >>puzz.spten times.  You could then use the command:.sp        crypto puzz.spto solve all ten puzzles at your leisure..PPHere is a complete list of the interactive commands available.  You canget a summary of this list with the command.I crypto -ior by typing '?' while running.I crypto\..PP.TP 13.B <let1><let2>guess the substitution cypher a letter at a time.  This is your primarytool in solving the cryptogram.  Example:  xb.TP 13.B <let><space>undefine <let>'s current guess.  <space> can be any non-letter.  Thislets you selectively remove your letters and try again..TP 13.B ^U<let>the alternate way to Undefine a letter's current guess..TP 13.B ^XUndefine ALL the guessed letters.  Good for starting afresh..TP 13.B ^CCheat -- if I know the solution, I will correct one letter in thepuzzle each time you decide to cheat..TP 13.B ^V<file>saVe the current puzzle, guess, and cypher information in a file.This allows you to continue where ever you left off withthe command crypto <file>.TP 13.B ^DDefine the current guess to be the solution.  Useful before a saveto define the cypher to save along with the puzzle (if not alreadyknown)..TP 13.B ^HHint -- select a word to perform a dictionary search on.  This isuseful for solving unknown cyphers.  All the currently guessedletters affect the matching of dictionary words into the cypher word.While in hint-mode, type '?' for a brief command summary..TP 13.B ^Ngo to the Next puzzle in a multi-puzzle file..TP 13.B ^Pgo to the Previous puzzle in a multi-puzzle file..TP 13.B ^Mpresents a Menu of the current puzzles available for selection..TP 13.B ^Atoggle the Alpha display between the two different output modes..TP 13.B ^Tenter a (new) Title for this puzzle..TP 13.B ^Q, <Esc>Quit -- leave the program.  Confirms the exit request if webelieve you might like to save the puzzle(s) first..TP 13.B ^L, ^RRedraw the screen..TP 13.B  6,  4Move a wide cryptogram left/right by 4 chars..TP 13.B  2,  8Move a long cryptogram up/down by 2 lines..TP 13.B  7,  1Move to the far left/right of a wide puzzle..TP 13.B  9,  3Move to the top/bottom of a long puzzle..PPThe following are valid command-line options:.TP 8.B \-uForce all input to Upper-case..TP 8.B \-eEncode the input stream with a random cypher..TP 8.B \-rencode using a simpler, rot-based random cypher.  You can specify a specificrotation by including a number from 1 to 26 after the r, as in:  -r13..TP 8.B \-cSTRencode using the Cypher STR for encryption.  STR is the 26 letters of thealphabet in whatever order you choose..TP 8.B \-gSTRinitialize the Guessed letters to STR..TP 8.B \-nindicates that No brace-commands are to be included in the save or pipeoutput.  Brace-commands are normally output to indicate the currentcypher, guess, and/or title..TP 8.B FILEread input from one or more FILEs, instead of stdin..TP 8.B \-ioutput a summary of the Interactive commands..TP 8.B \-houtput a summary of the command-line options..TP 8.B \-the null option can be used to avoid an empty command tail, whichwould output a short command-line summary..SH FILES/usr/dict/words.SH AUTHOR.I Cryptowas written by Wayne Davison.  Special thanx go to Bruce Holloway, whose"figure" program provided inspiration into curses & the dictionary lookup.